  "id": "hip-extension",
  "name": "Hip Extension Isolation",
  "definition": "A strict unilateral open-chain isolation movement in which the femur extends relative to a supported, position-held pelvis while the spine and knee positions remain held. The initial roster is limited to the directly reviewed prone-table fixture with the knee held at 90 degrees, the torso and pelvis supported by the table, and hip motion from 30 degrees of flexion to 5 degrees of extension. Gluteus maximus is the primary hip extensor; the medial hamstrings remain secondary despite their reduced contribution in the flexed-knee posture. The visible biceps-femoris region is credited only for stabilizing the held knee because its unsplit body-model mesh cannot distinguish the hip-extending long head from the monoarticular short head. Bridges, hip thrusts, hinges, quadruped kickbacks, standing cable or machine extensions, bands, external loads, straight-knee variants, and movements driven by the pelvis or spine remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "jeon-2016-prone-table-hip-extension"
  ],
